---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ESSAYS, HOW_PATH, SITE_LAB, adrUrl, howUrl } from '../../lib/links';

// The essay the Leases and TTLs principle maps to (docs/domain.md, "The principle map"; Q25 ·
// Q-ttl). The principle has no lab page yet, so the bakery explains it itself. Static, no
// island. Every claim about Cloudflare's behaviour is as its documentation stated it on the
// date in READ, and every number from the code names its file.
const READ = '2026-11-06';
const { title } = ESSAYS.leases;
const SOURCES = [
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/alarms/',
    title: 'Durable Objects alarms',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Durable Objects documentation',
    for: 'one alarm at a time per object, at-least-once execution, and retries with exponential backoff from 2 seconds, up to 6',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/sqlite-storage-api/',
    title: 'SQLite-backed Durable Object Storage',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Durable Objects documentation',
    for: 'deleteAll() also deletes an active alarm from a compatibility date of 2026-02-24, and point-in-time recovery reaches back 30 days',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing/',
    title: 'Durable Objects pricing',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Durable Objects documentation',
    for: 'setting an alarm and every delete are each a row written, 100,000 a day on Free and 50 million a month on Paid, then $1.00 a million',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/cron-triggers/',
    title: 'Cron Triggers',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Workers documentation',
    for: 'a cron trigger runs on UTC',
  },
  {
    href: SITE_LAB,
    title: 'The lab',
    by: 'the lab index on ihaveasegway.com',
    for: 'five lab pages, the Route, Queue, Cache, Backfill and Raft Labs, and none on leases, timeouts or expiry',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`${title} | Proof of Concept Bakery`}
  description="How Proof of Concept Bakery gives every held place and every sandbox an end written down at the start: the hold’s timer, the nightly wipe, the 36-hour backstop, and the late payment that goes past capacity."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A place in a pickup slot and a sandbox to play in are both borrowed. This is how each loan
      says when it ends, which object enforces that, what still ends a sandbox when the nightly wipe
      fails, and the one case where the end arrives after the money.
    </p>

    <h2>A place nobody paid for</h2>
    <p>
      A visitor orders two cardamom buns for the 10:15 pickup, reaches the checkout and closes the
      tab. The day’s Schedule object has already counted the order against the slot. Pickup slots
      take 6 orders by default (<code>docs/domain.md</code>, as built on 2026-11-05), so five more
      visitors can have 10:15 and the seventh is told <code>slot-full</code>, though the first order
      will never be paid. A sandbox has the same shape: a visitor who does not come back leaves tens
      of objects behind, about 2,500 rows written to seed them (<code>docs/adr/0005</code>
      and <code>docs/cost.md</code>, estimates as of 2026-11-04), and they are billed until
      something deletes them.
    </p>

    <h2>What a simpler design does, and where it breaks</h2>
    <p>
      The usual first design is a cleaner: a job that scans for holds and sandboxes that look
      abandoned and removes them. It needs a definition of abandoned, and every definition races
      with a visitor who is slow rather than gone. It is also one more thing that has to be running.
      If a run is missed, nothing ends, and nobody sees it until the slot is full of unpaid orders.
      No lab page shows this one yet. The{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        lab’s index
      </a>{' '}
      is where the lab explains the family of techniques around it; as read on {READ} it has five
      labs and none on expiry.
    </p>

    <h2>A promise with its end written down</h2>
    <p>
      The older answer is the lease. Gray and Cheriton’s 1989 paper on file-cache consistency grants
      a client rights for a fixed term, so that a server never has to find out whether the client is
      alive: when the term runs out, the rights are the server’s again. The bakery uses the idea
      more broadly than the paper. What matters is the order of events. The end is decided when the
      loan is made, so nothing ever has to discover that something was abandoned, and the object
      that granted the loan is the one that takes it back.
    </p>

    <h2>How the bakery writes the end down</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>A hold.</strong> Placing an order sends the Schedule a <code>Hold</code> whose{' '}
      <code>expires_at</code> is 15 minutes of business time after placement; the Order’s{' '}
      <code>held_until</code> is the same instant. In the transaction that writes the hold, the
      Schedule (<code>workers/api/src/objects/schedule.rs</code>) keeps a{' '}
      <code>hold:&lt;ref&gt;</code> row in its <code>timers</code> table and arms its one alarm for
      the earliest. When it fires, the object decides <code>Release</code> with the reason{' '}
      <code>hold_expired</code>, and the aggregate (<code>crates/domain/src/schedule.rs</code>)
      frees the place only if the hold is still unpaid and its time has come. A duplicate or early
      alarm changes nothing, which matters because Cloudflare’s alarms, as read on {READ}, run at
      least once and retry after a failure. A paid order’s timer is deleted in the same transaction
      as its confirmation.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Whose clock.</strong> A hold ends by business time, wall time plus the sandbox’s
      offset, so a visitor can advance the clock and watch every unpaid hold come due. A sandbox
      ends by wall time: <code>next_wipe_at</code> in <code>crates/app/src/wipe.rs</code> is the
      first 10:00 UTC after its birth, and the offset never moves it. Each end uses the clock of the
      thing it is about.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The wipe.</strong> The Sandbox has its own wipe timer, and the 10:00 UTC cron
      (Cloudflare’s cron triggers run on UTC, as read on {READ}) asks <code>Control</code> to stamp
      every sandbox not yet wiped as due. <code>Control</code> wipes 50 a run from its own alarm and
      re-arms while rows remain, and the 15-minute cron stamps again any that did not finish. Each
      run ends in the <code>wipe</code> data point.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The backstop.</strong> Every object arms a <code>backstop</code> timer 36 hours after
      its first write (<code>BACKSTOP_HOURS</code> in <code>wipe.rs</code>; <code>child.rs</code>).
      When it fires the object logs <code>backstop</code>, deletes its alarm and its storage. This
      is a lease on the object itself. The nightly wipe depends on <code>Control</code>’s ledger and
      the Sandbox’s registry; the backstop depends only on the object’s own alarm, so a sandbox the
      wipe missed still ends, at most 36 hours after its objects were born.
    </p>

    <h2>A retry after the end</h2>
    <p>
      A lease can also end between a visitor’s request and its retry. If a placement is retried
      under the same key after its hold was released, by the timer or by the router giving the place
      back when the Order refused, the day no longer replays a stale “held”. The router first asks
      whether the Order exists, and if it does the Order replays its own answer. If not, the
      Schedule decides <code>Retake</code> (<code>crates/domain/src/schedule.rs</code>): the same
      place is held again with the retry’s own 15 minutes when there is room, and otherwise the
      answer is 409 <code>hold-lost</code>, naming the next free slot. Taking it back rather than
      refusing it is the choice <code>docs/roadmap.md</code> records for the hardening of
      2026-11-06: the visitor asked once, and the place was free.
    </p>

    <h2>The payment that arrives after the end</h2>
    <p>
      The Order’s expiry and the Schedule’s hold timer are two timers on two objects at one instant,
      and either may fire first. Suppose the Schedule’s does, and the visitor’s payment is recorded
      while the Order is still <code>placed</code>. The Order accepts it and emits{' '}
      <code>OrderPaid</code>, and the Schedule’s <code>Confirm</code> finds the hold released.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bakery takes the place back, past capacity if it must, and the event says{' '}
      <code>revived: true</code>. Money has moved, and the alternative is to refuse the place and
      refund. That is what the Order does once its own expiry has run: a payment after it emits{' '}
      <code>OrderPaymentLate</code> and a refund request. The line between the two is where the
      Order’s state says the payment landed. <code>docs/roadmap.md</code> (PR 6) lists the revival
      as a reading for Joseph to confirm, and it is the one way a count passes its capacity.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A row and an alarm for every hold.</strong> Cloudflare’s pricing page, read on{' '}
        {READ}, bills each <code>setAlarm()</code> and each delete as a row written.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A slot that can hold 7 of 6.</strong> A revived place is the one count above
        capacity, and a visitor may see a slot that says full while it holds one more.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Up to 15 business minutes of a full slot.</strong> An order that will never be paid
        keeps its place until its hold ends.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An end the platform does not share.</strong> The sandbox is gone to the bakery, yet
        Cloudflare’s documentation, read on {READ}, keeps SQLite-backed storage recoverable for 30
        days, and nothing here calls the recovery (<code>docs/security.md</code>).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Order from <a href="/menu/">the menu</a> and stop at the checkout. Sign in, open{' '}
      <a href="/app/">the console</a>, advance the clock by 15 minutes, and read the capacity
      editor’s line for the slot: it says one fewer taken, and one more free. The console’s Deleted
      line counts down to the sandbox’s own end, and Wipe now ends it on the spot.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li>
        <em>
          Leases: An Efficient Fault-Tolerant Mechanism for Distributed File Cache Consistency
        </em>
        , Cary G. Gray and David R. Cheriton, the 12th ACM Symposium on Operating Systems
        Principles, 1989: a lease as rights for a fixed term, and the grantor’s freedom to take them
        back. Cited as <code>docs/architecture.md</code> lists it; the paper itself was not read on{' '}
        {READ}.
      </li>
      <li>
        <em>Release It!</em>, Michael T. Nygard, 2nd edition, 2018: the stability patterns, chapter
        5, among them timeouts, as the bakery’s <code>docs/architecture.md</code> cites them. The
        book was not read on {READ}.
      </li>
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">
            {s.title}
          </a>
          , {s.by}, read on {READ}: {s.for}.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="hint">
      Back to <a href={howUrl('leases')}>the leases principle on How it’s built</a>, to the decision
      that chose it,{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0005')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0005
      </a>
      , or to{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        the lab
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
